  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the minimum probability (interpreted as the highest single outcome probability) and prefers the lottery with the higher such value. Here, Lottery A has 90% and Lottery B has 55%, so the reason favors A. The individual also focuses on the high certainty (‘ziemlich sichere’) of 3000 Euros, which corresponds to the 90% probability in Lottery A, and compares it unfavorably to the perceived 50/50 nature of Lottery B. Although the individual simplifies Lottery B’s 45% to '50/50', they are clearly attending to the probability levels of the positive outcomes and prefer the one with higher certainty. This aligns closely with the core logic of the decision reason—choosing based on which lottery offers a higher probability of a non-zero outcome (or more precisely, the highest single outcome probability). The match between the information used (high probability of gain) and the justification (safety of 3000 Euros) and the reason’s rule (prefer higher minimum probability) is strong, though not perfectly precise due to the approximation of 45% as 50/50. Nevertheless, the central deliberation aligns with the reason’s structure.",
